# vacti distro-OWNED base packages (NOT engine arsenal)
#
# These packages are baked on every image but are NOT part of the engine's
# arsenal manifest (packaging/arsenal-manifest.json). They are the distro's own
# responsibility: base runtime, the headless Chromium shared-library closure,
# the anti-OOM backstops, the branded console banner, and a couple of TLS
# inspectors the distro chooses to carry. The ENGINE-ARSENAL half of every image
# is NOT listed here: it is DERIVED from the shared manifest at build time by
# gen-live-build-list.sh. Keeping the two concerns in separate files is what lets
# the arsenal half track the engine 1:1 while this distro-owned half stays stable.
#
# One package per line; blank lines and "#" comments are ignored (same contract
# gen-live-build-list.sh applies to the manifest-derived set).
#
# DO NOT add engine-arsenal (scanner/recon/exploitation) tools here: those belong
# in the engine's arsenal.env so every image AND the running fleet track them
# together. Add here only genuinely distro-specific, non-offensive packages.

# --- Base / runtime the appliance owns (not offensive tooling) ---
# wget: non-interactive downloader kept alongside curl (curl is engine core) for
# the occasional recipe/installer that assumes wget. Jev-classified distro_infra.
wget
# python3-full: full CPython stdlib metapackage; general scripting substrate the
# appliance relies on beyond the engine's own python3-venv. Jev: distro_infra.
python3-full

# --- Networking helper ---
# netcat-openbsd (nc): the engine manifest carries nc only under its CTF set, so
# a lean web/infra image would otherwise lack it. The distro keeps it baked on
# every image (reverse shells / manual OOB). Jev flags this as an ENGINE-ARSENAL
# gap (0.94): the engine arguably should promote nc into web/infra core, at which
# point it would arrive via the manifest and could be dropped from here. Until
# then it stays here so nothing currently installed disappears (superset rule).
netcat-openbsd

# --- TLS / network inspection ---
# sslscan + testssl.sh: lightweight TLS posture inspectors used by the tls-review
# path. Absent from the engine arsenal manifest, so the distro carries them. Jev
# leans these toward ENGINE-ARSENAL too (0.68 / 0.72) but at low confidence; they
# stay distro-owned until the engine adopts them, preserving the current set.
sslscan
testssl.sh

# --- Anti-OOM backstops (config drop-ins staged by golden-image/oom/) ---
# zram compressed RAM swap (primary cushion) + earlyoom userspace OOM killer
# backstop. Pure appliance infrastructure, nothing to do with the arsenal.
systemd-zram-generator
earlyoom

# --- Branded console banner ---
# fastfetch: the branded system-info banner shown over SSH (config + vacti logo
# shipped by the branding layer). Distro branding, not an engine tool.
fastfetch

# --- Headless Chromium shared-library closure ---
# The engine .deb bundles Chromium via playwright-core's pinned build; it is NOT
# a separate apt package, but its shared-library deps must be present on the thin
# Debian 13 genericcloud base or headless launch fails (the classic missing
# libnss3/libgbm1). Debian 13 (trixie) completed the 64-bit time_t transition, so
# several ship under t64 names. The per-package best-effort install tolerates a
# name absent on a given point release. These are OS libraries the distro owns,
# not arsenal tools.
libnss3
libgbm1
libasound2t64
libatk1.0-0t64
libatk-bridge2.0-0t64
libcups2t64
libdrm2
libxkbcommon0
libxcomposite1
libxdamage1
libxrandr2
libxfixes3
libpango-1.0-0
libcairo2
